window.__finlexFtShardLoaded("us_general_0",[[10182,"Overview","Scope & Statutory / Regulatory Role (Public Summary)","Landmark US federal legislation enacted following Enron/WorldCom that created the PCAOB, mandated CEO/CFO financial certifications (Sec. 302/906), established management and auditor ICFR attestation (Sec. 404), strengthened audit committee independence, and restricted auditor non-audit services."],[10182,"SOX Sec. 101, 201, 302, 404, 802, 906","PCAOB Oversight, CEO/CFO Certifications, ICFR (404) & Penalties","• Title I (Sec. 101–109): Established the PCAOB to inspect, regulate, and set auditing standards for public company auditors. • Title II (Sec. 201–209): Prohibits 9 categories of non-audit services for audit clients, mandates audit committee pre-approval, and requires 5-year lead/concurring audit partner rotation. • Section 302 & 906: Personal civil and criminal certifications by CEO and CFO on 10-K/10-Q/20-F accuracy and disclosure controls (up to $5M fine / 20 years imprisonment for willful false certification). • Section 404(a) & 404(b): Sec. 404(a) requires annual management assessment of ICFR; Sec. 404(b) requires independent auditor attestation under PCAOB AS 2201 (exempting non-accelerated filers and EGCs). • Section 301 & 806: Fully independent audit committees and whistleblower anti-retaliation protections."],[10182,"Cross-Framework Comparison","US vs. International (ISA/OECD/COSO) & China Context","• Global Ripple Effect: SOX 404 catalyzed China's《企业内部控制基本规范》&《企业内部控制审计指引》(C-SOX) and Japan's J-SOX, making COSO ICIF the de facto global ICFR benchmark."],[10182,"Official Access","Official Regulatory / Statutory Source","Official Source (SEC / U.S. Congress): https://www.sec.gov/rules-regulations/2003/03/managements-report-internal-control-over-financial-reporting-certification-disclosure-exchange-act Status: in_force."],[10183,"Overview","Scope & Statutory / Regulatory Role (Public Summary)","The twin statutory pillars of US federal securities regulation: the 1933 Act governs primary public offerings (IPOs — Forms S-1 / F-1, Section 11 strict liability), while the 1934 Act created the SEC and governs ongoing periodic reporting (Forms 10-K, 10-Q, 8-K, 20-F, 6-K) and Rule 10b-5 anti-fraud enforcement."],[10183,"1933 Act & 1934 Act (10-K / 20-F / 10b-5)","IPO Registration, Foreign Private Issuers (20-F) & Rule 10b-5","• Securities Act of 1933 (Primary Offerings): Requires registration statement (Form S-1 for domestic, Form F-1 for Foreign Private Issuers — FPIs) before public sale unless exempt (e.g., Reg D, Rule 144A); Section 11 imposes strict/negligence liability for material misstatements. • Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (Ongoing Reporting): Periodic filings — Form 10-K (annual, 60/75/90 days), Form 10-Q (quarterly), Form 8-K (current events within 4 business days); FPIs file annual Form 20-F (within 4 months, permitting US GAAP OR IASB IFRS without reconciliation!) and Form 6-K. • Section 10(b) & Rule 10b-5: Core anti-fraud & insider trading prohibition."],[10183,"Cross-Framework Comparison","US vs. International (ISA/OECD/COSO) & China Context","• Critical for Cross-Border Issuers: Since 2007, Foreign Private Issuers (including US-listed Chinese/European companies) may file Form 20-F using IFRS as issued by the IASB without US GAAP reconciliation, while still complying with SOX 302/404 and PCAOB audit standards."],[10183,"Official Access","Official Regulatory / Statutory Source","Official Source (U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)): https://www.sec.gov/rules-regulations/2003/03/managements-report-internal-control-over-financial-reporting-certification-disclosure-exchange-act Status: in_force."],[10184,"Overview","Scope & Statutory / Regulatory Role (Public Summary)","Prescribes the form, content, age, and audit requirements of financial statements filed with the SEC, including Rule 2-01 (Auditor Independence), Rule 3-05 (Significant Acquired Businesses), Rule 3-09 (Significant Equity Investees), and Article 11 (Pro Forma Financial Information)."],[10184,"Reg. S-X Art. 2, 3, 5, 10, 11","Auditor Independence (2-01), Significant Acquisitions (3-05) & Pro Forma","• Rule 2-01 (Auditor Independence): General standard ('reasonable investor' test) + strict rules on financial/employment/business relationships, prohibited non-audit services, and 5-year partner rotation. • Rule 3-05 & Rule 1-02(w) (Significant Acquisitions): Requires 1 or 2 years of audited financial statements for acquired businesses exceeding 20%/40% significance tests (Investment, Asset, Income/Revenue). • Article 5 (Commercial & Industrial Companies) & Article 10 (Interim 10-Q): Prescribes specific income statement/balance sheet captions. • Article 11 (Pro Forma): Transaction accounting adjustments for significant business acquisitions/dispositions."],[10184,"Cross-Framework Comparison","US vs. International (ISA/OECD/COSO) & China Context","• Bridges FASB ASC and SEC filings: Even when a company follows US GAAP (or IASB IFRS on Form 20-F), SEC Regulation S-X dictates financial statement age, significant acquiree financials, and auditor independence."],[10184,"Official Access","Official Regulatory / Statutory Source","Official Source (SEC / eCFR (17 CFR Part 210)): https://www.sec.gov/rules-regulations/2003/03/managements-report-internal-control-over-financial-reporting-certification-disclosure-exchange-act Status: in_force."],[10185,"Overview","Scope & Statutory / Regulatory Role (Public Summary)","Governs qualitative and non-financial disclosures in SEC registration statements and periodic reports, including Business (Item 101), Risk Factors (Item 105), Cybersecurity (Item 106), MD&A (Item 303), Executive Compensation (Item 402), and Non-GAAP Measures (Item 10(e))."],[10185,"Reg. S-K Item 10(e), 105, 106, 303, 402","MD&A (Item 303), Non-GAAP Rules (Item 10(e)) & Cybersecurity (106)","• Item 10(e) & Reg G (Non-GAAP Measures): Requires equal or greater prominence for the most directly comparable GAAP measure, quantitative reconciliation, and prohibition of misleading adjustments. • Item 303 (MD&A): Liquidity, capital resources, results of operations, known trends/uncertainties, and Critical Accounting Estimates. • Item 106 (Cybersecurity, effective Dec 2023): Annual disclosures of cybersecurity risk management, strategy, and board governance (paired with 4-business-day Form 8-K Item 1.05 material incident reporting). • Item 105 (Risk Factors) & Item 402 (Executive Compensation & Clawbacks)."],[10185,"Cross-Framework Comparison","US vs. International (ISA/OECD/COSO) & China Context","• Item 303 MD&A parallels IFRS Practice Statement 1 (Management Commentary), while Item 10(e) Non-GAAP rules parallel IFRS 18's new Management-Defined Performance Measures (MPMs)."],[10185,"Official Access","Official Regulatory / Statutory Source","Official Source (SEC / eCFR (17 CFR Part 229)): https://www.sec.gov/rules-regulations/2003/03/managements-report-internal-control-over-financial-reporting-certification-disclosure-exchange-act Status: in_force."],[10186,"Overview","Scope & Statutory / Regulatory Role (Public Summary)","Enacted to halt bribery of foreign government officials and off-the-books slush funds; jointly enforced by the DOJ (criminal) and SEC (civil) across two pillars: Anti-Bribery provisions (broad extraterritorial reach) and Accounting provisions (accurate books/records & internal accounting controls for all SEC issuers)."],[10186,"15 U.S.C. §78dd-1 / §78m(b)(2)","Anti-Bribery Prohibitions & Books-and-Records Accounting Provisions","• Anti-Bribery Provisions: Prohibits issuers, US domestic concerns, and foreign persons acting in US territory from corruptly offering/paying anything of value to a foreign official (including employees of state-owned enterprises) to obtain or retain business. • Accounting Provisions (Exchange Act §13(b)(2)): Requires ALL SEC issuers (including Foreign Private Issuers) to (a) make and keep books, records, and accounts in reasonable detail accurately reflecting transactions and dispositions of assets, and (b) devise and maintain a system of internal accounting controls. • No Materiality Threshold: Unlike financial statement fraud, FCPA books-and-records violations have NO financial statement materiality threshold."],[10186,"Cross-Framework Comparison","US vs. International (ISA/OECD/COSO) & China Context","• Directly reinforces COSO Internal Control, FATF anti-corruption/AML standards, and OECD MNE Guidelines Chapter VII (Combating Bribery), with major compliance impact on multinational subsidiaries and US-listed foreign issuers."],[10186,"Official Access","Official Regulatory / Statutory Source","Official Source (SEC & DOJ FCPA Resource Guide): https://www.sec.gov/spotlight/fcpa/fcpa-resource-guide.pdf Status: in_force."]]);
